            <ArticleTitle>Small area estimation of obesity prevalence and dietary patterns: a model applied to Rio de Janeiro city, Brazil.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>47-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.healthplace.2013.12.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1353-8292(13)00161-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We propose the use of previously developed small area estimation techniques to monitor obesity and dietary habits in developing countries and apply the model to Rio de Janeiro city. We estimate obesity prevalence rates at the Census Tract through a combinatorial optimization spatial microsimulation model that matches body mass index and socio-demographic data in Brazil's 2008-9 family expenditure survey with Census 2010 socio-demographic data. Obesity ranges from 8% to 25% in most areas and affects the poor almost as much as the rich. Male and female obesity rates are uncorrelated at the small area level. The model is an effective tool to understand the complexity of the problem and to aid in policy design.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2013 Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
